## Why This Is Asked

Models real build/dependency graphs where costs accumulate; asks for both memoized and tabular DP to compare patterns and understand trade-offs. Emphasizes modulo arithmetic and edge-case handling for unreachable sinks and tight budgets.

## Key Concepts

- DP on DAG with an extra weight dimension
- Top-down memoization with (node, remainingBudget)
- Bottom-up DP in topological order with weight axis
- Modulo arithmetic and edge-case handling

## Code Example

```javascript
// Top-down memoized DFS
function countPaths(n, edges, W) {
  const adj = Array.from({length: n}, () => []);
  for (const [u, v, w] of edges) adj[u].push([v, w]);
  const memo = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(W + 1).fill(-1));
  const dfs = (u, rem) => {
    if (u === n - 1) return 1;
    if (memo[u][rem] !== -1) return memo[u][rem];
    let res = 0;
    for (const [v, w] of adj[u]) if (w <= rem) res = (res + dfs(v, rem - w)) % 1_000_000_007;
    return memo[u][rem] = res;
  };
  return dfs(0, W);
}
```

```javascript
// Bottom-up DP in topological order
function countPathsBottomUp(n, edges, W) {
  const adj = Array.from({length: n}, () => []);
  const indeg = Array(n).fill(0);
  for (const [u, v, w] of edges) {
    adj[u].push([v, w]);
    indeg[v]++;
  }
  // Kahn's algorithm for topo order
  const q = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) if (indeg[i] === 0) q.push(i);
  const topo = [];
  while (q.length) {
    const u = q.shift(); topo.push(u);
    for (const [v] of adj[u]) if (--indeg[v] === 0) q.push(v);
  }
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(W + 1).fill(0));
  dp[0][0] = 1;
  for (const u of topo) {
    for (const [v, w] of adj[u]) {
      for (let t = 0; t <= W - w; t++) {
        if (dp[u][t] !== 0) dp[v][t + w] = (dp[v][t + w] + dp[u][t]) % 1_000_000_007;
      }
    }
  }
  let ans = 0;
  for (let t = 0; t <= W; t++) ans = (ans + dp[n - 1][t]) % 1_000_000_007;
  return ans;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize memory if W is very large but reachable weights are sparse? 
- What changes if edges can be negative but the graph is still acyclic?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to combine hash maps, deques, and heaps to implement a sliding-window top-K feature in real time.

## Key Concepts
- Sliding window with per-player aggregates
- Lazy deletions in a heap to avoid frequent rebalancing
- Expiration management via per-player deques
- Trade-offs between memory usage and update/query latency

## Code Example
```javascript
class Leaderboard {
  constructor(W) {
    this.W = W;
    this.map = new Map();
    this.heap = [];
  }

  addEvent(pid, delta, ts) {
    const record = this.map.get(pid) || { sum: 0, deque: [] };
    record.deque.push({ ts, delta });
    record.sum += delta;

    while (record.deque.length && record.deque[0].ts < ts - this.W) {
      record.sum -= record.deque.shift().delta;
    }

    this.map.set(pid, record);
    this.heap.push({ sum: record.sum, pid });
  }

  topK(now, K) {
    const results = [];
    for (let i = 0; i < K; i++) {
      while (this.heap.length) {
        const top = this.heap[0];
        const record = this.map.get(top.pid);
        if (!record || record.sum !== top.sum) {
          heapPop(this.heap);
        } else {
          results.push(this.heapPop(this.heap).pid);
          break;
        }
      }
    }
    return results;
  }
}
```

## Data Structure Overview
| Component | Purpose | Complexity |
|-----------|---------|-------------|
| Hash Map | O(1) lookup per player | O(n) memory |
| Per-player Deque | Track event history, expire old entries | O(1) amortized push/shift |
| Max-Heap | Maintain top players efficiently | O(log n) push, O(1) peek |

## Time & Space Complexity
- **addEvent**: O(log n) amortized—each event enters and leaves the deque at most once
- **topK**: O(K log n)—each invalid entry is popped at most once across all queries
- **Memory**: O(n) for the hash map and deques; O(n) worst-case for the heap

## Follow-up Questions
- How to tighten the lazy deletion cost when the heap grows large?
- Can a segment tree or Fenwick tree provide stricter time guarantees?
- How would you handle concurrent updates from multiple streams?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP understanding with obstacles and modular arithmetic; ensures edge-case handling and ability to implement both bottom-up and top-down approaches.

## Key Concepts
- DP with blockers (Fibonacci variant)
- Bottom-up vs top-down memoization
- Space optimization (O(1) possible with two variables)
- Modulo handling with BigInt or large integers

## Edge Cases
- **Start blocked (step 0)**: Return 0 immediately — no valid paths exist
- **End blocked (step N)**: dp[N] remains 0 since we cannot land on N
- **Consecutive blocked steps**: Creates a barrier; all steps after a chain of 2+ blocked steps will have dp[i]=0 (cannot be reached)

## Code Example
```javascript
const MOD = 1000000007n;

// Bottom-up DP: O(N) time, O(N) space
function countWaysBottomUp(blocked, n) {
  if (blocked[0]) return 0; // start blocked

  const dp = new Array(n + 1).fill(0n);
  dp[0] = 1n;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    if (blocked[i]) {
      dp[i] = 0n; // blocked step: no ways to land here
      continue;
    }
    dp[i] = (dp[i-1] + (i >= 2 ? dp[i-2] : 0n)) % MOD;
  }
  return Number(dp[n]);
}

// Top-down memoization: O(N) time, O(N) space
function countWaysTopDown(blocked, n) {
  if (blocked[0]) return 0; // start blocked

  const memo = new Array(n + 1).fill(-1n);

  function solve(i) {
    if (i < 0) return 0n;    if (i === 0) return 1n;
    if (blocked[i]) return 0n; // this step is blocked
    if (memo[i] !== -1n) return memo[i];

    memo[i] = (solve(i-1) + solve(i-2)) % MOD;
    return memo[i];
  }

  return Number(solve(n));
}

// Space-optimized: O(N) time, O(1) space
function countWaysOptimized(blocked, n) {
  if (blocked[0]) return 0;

  let prev2 = 1n, prev1 = blocked[1] ? 0n : 1n;

  for (let i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
    const curr = blocked[i] ? 0n : (prev1 + prev2) % MOD;
    prev2 = prev1;
    prev1 = curr;
  }

  return Number(n === 0 ? prev2 : prev1);
}
```

## Complexity Analysis
- **Time**: O(N) for all variants — each step computed once
- **Space**: O(N) for basic DP/memoization, O(1) for optimized version
- **Modulo**: Applied at each addition to prevent overflow

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to combine arrays/linked lists (deques), hash maps, and a heap to maintain a sliding-window top-K efficiently.

## Key Concepts
- Sliding window, expiration, lazy deletions in a heap
- Per-user state via linked-list/deque and running sums
- Time/space trade-offs for real-time top-K

## Code Example
```javascript
class WaveTopK {
  constructor(W) {
    this.W = W;
    this.userMap = new Map(); // user_id -> {queue: [], sum}
    this.heap = new MaxHeap(); // stores [sum, user_id]
  }
  addEvent(userId, score, ts) {
    let entry = this.userMap.get(userId);
    if (!entry) {
      entry = { queue: [], sum: 0 };
      this.userMap.set(userId, entry);
    }
    entry.queue.push([ts, score]);
    entry.sum += score;
    this.heap.push([entry.sum, userId]);
  }
  topK(currentTime, K) {
    const seen = new Map();
    const res = [];
    while (res.length < K && !this.heap.isEmpty()) {
      const [sum, uid] = this.heap.pop();
      const ent = this.userMap.get(uid);
      if (!ent) continue;
      // expire check
      this.expireUser(uid, currentTime);
      if (ent.sum !== sum) {
        this.heap.push([ent.sum, uid]);
        continue;
      }
      res.push(uid);
    }
    // Reinsert popped elements
    for (const uid of res) this.heap.push([this.userMap.get(uid).sum, uid]);
    return res;
  }
  expireUser(userId, currentTime) {
    const ent = this.userMap.get(userId);
    if (!ent) return;
    while (ent.queue.length && ent.queue[0][0] <= currentTime - this.W) {
      const [, val] = ent.queue.shift();
      ent.sum -= val;
    }
  }
}
```

